# Carom

## Overview

Carom is a neon, top-down paddle duel for the browser. Two paddles face each
other across a dark field while a ball ricochets between them, off the top and
bottom walls, and off a pair of obstacles in the middle of the field. A player
scores when the ball passes the far edge behind their opponent's paddle.

Carom is a duel of angles. Its defining mechanic is spin: the motion of a paddle
at the moment it strikes the ball curves the ball's flight afterward, so skilled
play is about shaping the ball's path rather than simply blocking it. The
obstacles turn the open field into a bank-shot puzzle.

Carom has its own name, look, spin mechanic, and obstacle layout. Do not
reproduce the assets, branding, or design of any existing game.

## How the specification is organized

This specification is split across several files that cross-reference each other
by name. Read all of them and build the game they describe as one cohesive whole.

- `specs/overview.md` — this file: the overview, the project you build in, the
  coordinate system, and the visual design.
- `specs/playfield.md` — the playfield, paddles, obstacles, and ball, including
  how the obstacles behave and how the ball collides with them.
- `specs/balls.md` — the balls and serving, and the physics: how many balls are
  in play, where they spawn and how they are served, the physics loop and
  collision, and the paddle bounce and spin mechanic.
- `specs/ui.md` — the menus, screens, and navigation between them, plus scoring
  and match flow and audio.
- `specs/modes/single-player.md` — the Solo way to play: its controls, the HUD,
  and the AI opponent.
- `specs/modes/versus.md` — the Versus way to play: its controls and the HUD.
- `specs/instrumentation.md` — the `window.__carom` debugging and automation
  surface, and what the game's state must hold for it to work.

The game is built on the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents itself at
`{{engine.docs}}` rather than in this specification. Read it before you start: it
defines the frame loop and the delta time the game is given, input actions,
audio cues, asset loading, the debug overlay, and how a game built on it is
tested. This specification states only what is specific to Carom.

## The project you build in

The project at the root of this repository is already wired up, and its `README.md`
describes it. It is a TypeScript project built with Vite, tested with Vitest,
linted with ESLint, and formatted with Prettier. That toolchain is fixed and
supplied: it is not a choice, and it is not yours to change.

### What you implement

`src/game.ts`, and any new modules you add beside it under `src/`.

`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `Game<CaromState>`: `initialize` builds the state
once, `update` advances it against the frame's delta time in seconds, and `render`
draws it. All three throw `"not implemented"` as they stand. Implement them, and
split the work across new files under `src/` however suits you.

`CaromState`, declared in full in `src/game.ts`, is a contract. Keep every field
under its declared name, type, and meaning. You may add fields, but only for data
you can rebuild from the declared ones: the declared fields are the whole of the
authoritative state, and they are what `specs/instrumentation.md` reads and poses.
Hold no game state anywhere else — no module-level variables, no closures over
mutable data.

### What you must not edit

- `src/main.ts` — the entry point. It creates the engine over the page's canvas,
  binds `game` to it, installs the debug surface, and runs.
- `src/constants.ts` — every figure this specification fixes.
- `src/debug.ts` — the `window.__carom` surface described in
  `specs/instrumentation.md`, supplied already written.
- `index.html` — the page and the canvas the engine fits the field into.
- The configuration: `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`,
  `vitest.config.ts`, `eslint.config.js`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`,
  and `.gitignore`.

Add a dependency to `package.json` if you genuinely need one, and commit the
updated `package-lock.json`. Leave the existing entries and scripts alone.

### Every figure comes from `src/constants.ts`

Every number, color, key binding, cue name, and piece of screen copy this
specification fixes is exported from `src/constants.ts` under the name this
specification gives it. Import it and use it. Do not restate a figure as a
literal in your own code, and do not derive a second copy of one.

Where a spec states a value it also names the constant that holds it, and the
constant is authoritative. Anything this specification does not name is yours to
decide.

### Your own tests

Write unit tests for what you build, beside your sources as `src/**/*.test.ts`.
`npm test` runs them in process with Vitest, in Node, with coverage measured over
`src/`. There is no browser and no DOM: a test builds the engine over its own
canvas and clock and steps the game with `engine.advance`, exactly as the engine's
documentation at `{{engine.docs}}` shows. Cover the parts of the game a person
cannot see at a glance — the collision resolution, the paddle bounce and spin, the
serve, the scoring and match rules, and the AI.

### The checks run over what you deliver

Four commands run over the finished repository, and their results are recorded
with the run:

| Command | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `npx tsc --noEmit` | Gating. Code that does not compile scores zero. |
| `npx eslint .` | Recorded. |
| `npx prettier --check .` | Recorded. |
| `npx vitest run --coverage` | Recorded, with the test count and the coverage. |

They run on the repository as you leave it, so leave it type-clean, lint-clean,
and formatted, with your tests passing. `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint`,
`npm run format`, and `npm test` run the same four locally.

### The build interface

`npm ci` followed by `npm run build` produces the complete static site, with no
further manual step, into `dist/` at the repository root, with an `index.html` at
the root of that directory as the entry point. That directory runs correctly when
served as-is at the root of any static file server, since it is deployed to static
hosting exactly that way. The scripts that do this are already in `package.json`.

## Goal of this build

Produce a complete, polished, playable game that runs entirely in a browser.
This is a substantial front-end build: real-time rendered graphics, a physics
loop, multiple game states and menus, an AI opponent, and a local two-player
mode. Aim for a game a person would enjoy playing, not a tech demo.

### Hard requirements

- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine. The build must use it rather than write
  its own frame loop, input handling, audio, or debug overlay. Its contract is
  documented at `{{engine.docs}}`.
- Render real graphics, drawing the game through the engine. A text-only or ASCII
  rendering does not satisfy this requirement.
- Draw everything in code. The build ships no art or audio files and fetches
  nothing at runtime.
- Run in the browser with no backend. No server, accounts, database, or network
  calls at runtime. Everything needed to play is self-contained.
- No API keys or credentials of any kind to build, run, or play.
- Hold the game's whole state in `CaromState`, so the surface in
  `specs/instrumentation.md` can pose and read it.
- Replace the starter `README.md` with one explaining what the game is, how to
  install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the static
  production build, and the controls.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions, sizes, and speeds in this specification are given in logical pixels
on a fixed play area of `FIELD_W x FIELD_H` (`1280 x 720`, 16:9). The origin
`(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the right and `y` increases downward.
The field center is `(FIELD_CX, FIELD_CY)` (`640, 360`).

- `FIELD_W x FIELD_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the browser
  window — the uniform scale that preserves the aspect ratio, the letterboxed
  centering on the background color, and the device pixel ratio — is the engine's,
  so the complete field is on screen at every window size, on load and at any
  pixel density, without the build doing anything about it.
- Draw in logical pixels, and never read the canvas element's own size.
- Gameplay logic operates in logical-pixel space, independent of the rendered
  scale.

Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in seconds,
integrated against the delta time the engine hands each update. Nothing in this
game counts frames.

## Visual design

The look is neon-on-charcoal. Every color below is exported from
`src/constants.ts` on the `COLOR` object.

| Element | Constant | Color |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Field background | `COLOR.bg` | `#0b0e14` |
| Raised panel fill | `COLOR.bgRaised` | `#11151f` |
| Player one paddle | `COLOR.p1` | `#3ae7c4` |
| Player two / AI paddle | `COLOR.p2` | `#ff5c8a` |
| Ball | `COLOR.ball` | `#f2f5f7` |
| Obstacles | `COLOR.obstacle` | `#ffb454` |
| Center net | `COLOR.net` | `#243044` |
| Primary text | `COLOR.text` | `#e6edf3` |
| Secondary text | `COLOR.textDim` | `#8a94a6` |
| Faint text | `COLOR.textFaint` | `#4a5567` |
| Panel border | `COLOR.panelBorder` | `#20283a` |

- Draw all text in the `MONO` font stack. It is a system monospace stack, so the
  game renders identically offline with no downloaded web font.
- Paddles, the ball, and obstacles have a soft neon glow, each drawn in its color
  above. The interior of each of those shapes is that exact color, so the glow is
  a halo around a solid body rather than a wash over it.
- The center net is a dashed vertical line at `x = NET_X` (`640`), drawn in
  `COLOR.net`. It is decoration and has no collision.
- The ball leaves a motion trail. Behind the moving ball, draw a fading tail that
  traces its recent path, so it visibly curves whenever spin curves the flight.
  It reads as one continuous streak, a smooth comet rather than a row of discrete
  dots, and tapers from the ball: widest and brightest where it meets the ball,
  then narrowing and fading smoothly to nothing at its oldest end. The trail
  represents `TRAIL_TIME` (`0.13`) seconds of recent travel, so its length is
  proportional to the ball's current speed. It stretches as the ball speeds up,
  shortens as it slows, and collapses to almost nothing during the pre-serve hold
  while the ball is still. Draw it in `COLOR.ball`. The trail is what makes the
  ball's speed and spin legible in motion.
- The title screen, the in-match view, and the match-over screen are described in
  full under the screens in `specs/ui.md`. Implement each in this palette and
  type.
